% Liveness, the textbook's equations as rules (docs/plans/plan_prolog.md,
% stage 9), over the facts of mini-cc -flow (Ir_facts.mli): a variable
% is live into an instruction that reads it, or that it is live out of
% and that does not write it; live out of one when live into a
% successor. The same sets as mini-cc's Opti's own (its regs pass).
%   live_in(V, P), live_out(V, P)
%   across_call(V, P): live after a call, what a register would cost there
% (For a function in SSA form, mini-ml's: liveness_ssa.dl.)
%
% cs-history:
% Gary Kildall's dataflow framework (POPL 1973): a lattice of facts at
% each point, equations between points, solved by iteration to their
% fixpoint; liveness is its backward instance, as old as register
% allocation by coloring (Gregory Chaitin, 1982). That those equations
% are Datalog rules and the iteration Datalog's evaluation was
% Thomas Reps's observation (1994, demand analyses by magic sets);
% Datalog's sets are the lattice when the facts are sets, as here.
% Where they are not (a constant's value, an interval), Datalog needs
% lattices: Flix's (Magnus Madsen, Ming-Ho Yee, Ondrej Lhotak, PLDI
% 2016) and Soufflé's later extensions.
%
% References: Gary Kildall, "A unified approach to global program
% optimization" (POPL 1973); Thomas Reps, "Solving demand versus
% exhaustive dataflow using magic sets" (1994); Magnus Madsen and
% others, "From Datalog to Flix" (PLDI 2016).

live_in(V, P) :- use(V, P).
live_in(V, P) :- live_out(V, P), \+ def(V, P).
live_out(V, P) :- succ(P, Q), live_in(V, Q).
across_call(V, P) :- call(P), live_out(V, P).
